.1
Menu Bar 
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The menu bar provides the means for all simulator windows to be chosen and 
subsequently displayed.  At the top of the screen are a list of categories.
Upon selecting one of the categories at the top of screen, a drop down submenu 
containing choices relevant to the selected category will appear.  These choices
typically specify windows to open or actions to occur.  Selecting one of these
options will either open the chosen window or cause the simulator to take the 
given action. 

Note: The <ESC> key will cancel a menu selection.  Press <ESC> twice if a
submenu is displayed, but just press <ESC> once if only the menu bar itself is
selected.  

These are the categories of the menu bar:

 @179[File] @180[Register]  @181[Memory]  @182[Stacks]  @183[Commands]  @184[Execution]  @185[Display]  @186[Misc]  @178[Help]  @187[Quit]
 
.2
Exit
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Exit function provides a means to leave the simulator and return to the
operating system.  The current display configuration, this includes window
sizes, positions, colors and floating point display attributes are written out
to the last window file that was loaded.  If no window file was loaded, the
configuration is saved to a file which the simulator will read in by default.

A pop-up window appears prompting the user for confirmation before the simulator
exits. 

See also @3[Quit].
.3
Quit
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Quit function provides a means to leave the simulator and return to the
operating system.  No display configuration data is saved.

A pop-up window appears prompting the user for confirmation before the simulator
exits. 

See also @2[Exit].
.4
Abort 
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Abort function actually provides several functions depending upon the
current context.  It is usually invoked by pressing the <ESC> key.

   - When invoked from the @175[help system], this function will return the user to 
the simulator.

   - When invoked from the active window, this function will close the active
window.

   - When invoked from the @1[menu bar], this function will cancel any selections
made from the menu.

   - When invoked from a pop-up, this function will close the pop-up and cancel
any actions incurred by the pop-up.

.5
Next Window
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function allows the user to move between windows.  This function is invoked
by the @177[<F2>] key.  
.6
Redraw Display
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function simply redraws the entire screen.  This function is invoked from
the @1[menu bar].   
.7
Center Windows
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will center all open windows to the middle of the screen.  It is 
typically used when some window(s) has been moved off the screen.  This function
is invoked from the @1[menu bar]. 
.8
List Windows
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will produce a pop-up window containing a list of all open
windows.  The user can select from the list a window to transfer control into or
simply remain in the current window.

This function can be invoked from the @1[menu bar] or by pressing @177[<Ctrl-L>].

.9
Move Window Up
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the top of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@177[<F5>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.
.10
Move Window Down
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the bottom of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing
the @177[<F6>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.
.11
Move Window Left
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the left side of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing
the @177[<F7>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.
.12
Move Window Right
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the right side of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing
the @177[<F8>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.
.14
Make Window Taller
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will vertically increase the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@177[<Shift-F5>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.
.15
Make Window Shorter
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will vertically decrease the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@177[<Shift-F6>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.
.16
Make Window Wider
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will horizontally increase the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@177[<Shift-F7>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.
.17
Make Window Narrower
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will horizontally decrease the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@177[<Shift-F8>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.
.18
Toggle Window Format
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will toggle the current display format of the current window, 
assuming the window is capable of supporting multiple formats.  For example,
when the current window is a Program Memory window, this function will
change the display format from it's current format to either hexadecimal, 
1.15 fixed point, disassembled.  

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<Ctrl-T>] key.

.19
Video Mode
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function allows the user to change the video mode of the display.  The
simulator supports 3 video modes, all of which are text modes:

                 - 80 Columns x 25 Rows (default)
                 - 80 Columns x 43 Rows (EGA and VGA only)
                 - 80 Columns x 50 Rows (VGA only)

The simulator first determines which modes are supported on the hardware 
platform and then pops up a selection menu containing a list of choices based
upon the hardware configuration.  The video mode is saved in and loaded from
the the window file.  This function is invoked from the the @1[menu bar]. 

See also @98[Save Window File] and @97[Load Window File].

(NOTE: This feature is supported on PC platforms only.)
.20
About Simulator...
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function displays information about the simulator.  This function is
invoked from the the @1[menu bar]. 


.21
DAG Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

These are the Data Address Generator  (DAG) registers.  The DAGs are used to
generate memory addresses.  There are 4 sets of 14 bit registers in each DAG.

                    Index Registers:   I0 thru I3 DAG1  I4 thru I7 DAG2
                    Modify Registers:  M0 thru M3 DAG1  M4 thru M7 DAG2
                    Length Registers:  L0 thru L3 DAG1  L4 thru L7 DAG2

The DAG registers can be displayed in hexadecimal and decimal formats. 
Note that in the decimal display format the I and L registers are
displayed as 14 bit unsigned values whereas the M registers are displayed in 14
bit signed two's complement format.  The current format can be changed by 
pressing @18[<Ctrl-T>].

.23
Computational Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of the computational registers.  These registers are the
foreground or background ALU and MAC registers. The selection of primary and
secondary sets is made via the @33[MSTAT] register.

The registers can be displayed in hexadecimal, decimal 
and 1.15 fixed point formats.
The current format can be changed by pressing @18[<Ctrl-T>].
.29
Memory Mapped Control Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The control registers consist of the wait state registers, (PWAIT and DWAIT0
thru DWAIT4), the timer control registers ,(timer scale, timer period, and
timer count), the sport 0 and sport 1 enables, and the boot control 
registers (boot force, boot page, boot wait, and boot mode for 2111 and 2150).
The Rom Enable register is also here for the 2150 simulator. All these values
may also be modified in the @56[data memory window].
.31
Program Control Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The program control registers include the program counter (PC), the counter
register (CNTR), the cycle count (CYCLE), the last DM address accessed 
(DM_ADDR), and the last PM address accessed (PM_ADDR). The cycle count and
the PM and DM address registers are NOT real registers which exist in the
2100 family. These are provided for convenience and debugging help.
.33
Status Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The arithmetic status (ASTAT), the mode status register (MSTAT), and the
stack status register (SSTAT) are broken down in this window.
The Arithmetic Status Register (ASTAT) information is generated by the 
computational units.  The bits in ASTAT are defined as follows:

   Bit    Name    Definition
    0     AZ      ALU result zero or floating point underflow
    1     AV      ALU overflow
    2     AN      ALU result negative
    3     AC      ALU fixed point carry
    4     AS      ALU X input sign 
    5     AQ      ALU quotient sign
    6     MV      MAC overflow
    7     SS      Shifter input sign

@33[more]
The bits in the MSTAT register are defined as follows:

   Bit    Name    Definition
    0     RBANK   0 primary, 1 secondary registers
    1     BIT_R   1 = bit reverse operation
    2     ALUOV   ALU overflow latch mode
    3     ARSAT   AR saturation mode
    4     MACRP   MAC result placement mode (not 2100)
    5     T_ENA   Timer enable (not 2100)
    6     G_ENA   Go Mode enabled (not 2100)

The bits in the SSTAT register are defined as follows:

   Bit    Name    Definition
    0     PC_E    PC stack empty        see also @105[PC stack]
    1     PC_O    PC stack overflow     see also @105[PC stack]
    2     CS_E    Count stack empty     see also @107[Loop stack]
    3     CS_O    Count stack overflow  see also @107[Loop Stack]
    4     SS_E    Status stack empty    see also @106[Status stack]
    5     SS_O    Status stack overflow see also @106[Status stack]
    6     LS_E    Loop stack empty      see also @108[Loop address]
    7     LS_O    Loop stack overflow   see also @108[Loop address]
.34
Flag Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The flag registers consist of a flag-in pin and a flag-out pin. The 2111
and 2150 simulators also have three additional flag registers -- FL0,FL1,
and FL2.

.35
Interrupt Control Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Interrupt Control Registers consist of the interrupt requested 
register (IREQ), the interrupt mask register (IMASK), and the interrupt 
control register (ICNTL). All simulators except for the 2100 also have
the interrupt force register (IFC).
IREQ is not a real 2100 family register. It shows which interrupts are 
pending and is provided for convenience. The bits in IREQ map with the bits
in IMASK.

The bits in the ICNTL register are defined as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       IRQ0 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge
    1       IRQ1 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge
    2       IRQ2 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge
    3       IRQ3 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge (2100 only)
    4       Interrupt Nesting mode

@35[more]
The bits in the IFC register are defined as follows:
   (2101&2111)
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer interrupt clear
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt clear
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt clear
    3       SPORT 0 receive interrupt clear
    4       SPORT 0 transmit interrupt clear
    5       IRQ2 interrupt clear

    6       Timer interrupt force
    7       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt force
    8       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt force
    9       SPORT 0 receive interrupt force
    10      SPORT 0 transmit interrupt force
    11      IRQ2 interrupt force

@35[more]
The IFC bit definitions for 2150 are defined as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer interrupt clear
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt clear
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt clear
    3       CODEC  receive interrupt clear
    4       CODEC  transmit interrupt clear
    5       SPORT 0 receive interrupt clear
    6       SPORT 0 transmit interrupt clear
    7       IRQ2 interrupt clear

    8       Timer interrupt force
    9       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt force
    10      SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt force
    11      CODEC receive interrupt force
    12      CODEC transmit interrupt force
    13      SPORT 0 receive interrupt force
    14      SPORT 0 transmit interrupt force
    15      IRQ2 interrupt force

@35[more]
The bits in the IMASK register are defined for the 2100 as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       IRQ0 Enable
    1       IRQ1 Enable
    2       IRQ2 Enable
    3       IRQ3 Enable

The bits in the IMASK register are defined for the 2101 as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer interrupt enable
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt enable
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt enable
    3       SPORT 0 receive interrupt enable
    4       SPORT 0 transmit interrupt enable
    5       IRQ2 interrupt enable

@35[more]
The bits in the IMASK register are defined for the 2111 as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer interrupt enable
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt enable
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt enable
    3       SPORT 0 receive interrupt enable
    4       SPORT 0 transmit interrupt enable
    5       HIP read interrupt enable
    6       HIP write interrupt enable
    7       IRQ2 interrupt enable

@35[more]
The bits in the IMASK register are defined for the 2150 as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer interrupt enable
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt enable
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt enable
    3       CODEC receive interrupt enable
    4       CODEC transmit interrupt enable
    5       SPORT 0 receive interrupt enable
    6       SPORT 0 transmit interrupt enable
    7       HIP read interrupt enable
    8       HIP write interrupt enable
    9       IRQ2 interrupt enable

@35[first screen]
.36
PX Register
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The PX register is used for 24 bit data transfers. Essentially, the LSB of
data from a read of PM data is loaded into PX. The LSB of data when writing 
to PM data memory is written from the PX register.
.37
CE Register
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The CE register is not a real register. It reports the state of the counter 
empty logic from inside the chip. It is brought out here for debugging
purposes.
.38
Sport Control Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The SPORT control registers are broken out by SPORT in these windows.
(0,1) refers to SPORT 0 or SPORT 1.
The following chart describes these registers:

Name                  Description
SLEN(0,1)             serial length of data
DTYPE(0,1)            data type (format of the serial data)
ISCLK(0,1)            sclk required flag
IRFS(0,1)             internal receive frame synch 
ITFS(0,1)             internal transmit frame synch 
RFSR(0,1)             receive frame synch required flag
TFSR(0,1)             transmit frame synch required flag
RFSW(0,1)             receive frame synch width
TFSW(0,1)             transmit frame synch width
SCLKDIV(0,1)          sclk divisor (use to set up frequency)
RFSDIV(0,1)           receive frame synch divisor 
@38[more]
RBUF(0,1)             receive autobuffering enable
TBUF(0,1)             transmit autobuffering enable
RIREG(0,1)            receive autobuffering I register
TIREG(0,1)            transmit autobuffering I register
RMREG(0,1)            receive autobuffering M register
TMREG(0,1)            transmit autobuffering M register
MCRE0                 multichannel receive word zero enables
MCRE1                 multichannel receive word one enables
MCTE0                 multichannel transmit word zero enables
MCTE1                 multichannel transmit word one enables
MCE0                  multichannel enable
MCEFD                 multichannel frame delay
RX(0,1)               receive register          
TX(0,1)               transmit register          
@38[first page]
.42
Serial Port input/output files
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

To simulate a serial port, you open it, assigning an input file and / or
an output file to it. You can also examine and change the values of 
registers associated with SPORT operation. This section describes commands
and windows used in SPORT simulation. See @38[Sport Registers].

SPORTs (serial ports must be explicitly opened to be simulated. When you open 
a SPORT, you associate it with one or two data files. An input data file serves
as the source for simulated serial input. An output data file serves as the 
destination for simulated serial output. You can analyze these data files after 
simulation to assess the processing of your algorithm.

The data input files for SPORT simulation must contain only ASCII 1s and 0s to
simulate the serial bit stream, and carriage returns (which are ignored).
.43
Host Interface Port  Registers
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

To simulate the the host interface port of the ADSP-2111 and ADSP-21msp50,
you can examine and alter the contents of registers directly. You simulate
host writes by writing to a data register (HDR). You can also associate
data files with the HIP to simulate host data transfers. See @45[HIP Files].

By convention, the simulator calls a data transfer from the host a write
and a transfer to the host a read; in other words, "write" and "read"
are from the host's perspective.

The top portion of the HIP Register Window is divided into three fields:
the Edit field, the 21xx field (representing either the ADSP-2111 or the
ADSP-21msp50), and the Host field. The six HIP data registers (HDRs) appear
in each of the three fields. You can enter either a data value or type
CTRL_R (for read) in an HDR.
@43[more]

-- Typing a value in in the Host field simulates a write to the ADSP-21xx;
   the HDR is changed and a write interrupt is generated.

-- Typing a value in the 21xx field has the same effect as an immediate
   data move to an HDR from the ADSP-21xx.

-- Typing CTRL_R (control R) in the Host field simulates a Host read of
   the HDR; the ADSP-21xx generates a read interrupt.

-- Typing CTRL_R (control R) in the 21xx field simulates the effect of
   the ADSP-21xx reading the HDR (the data is not transferred anywhere,
   but the HDR's status changes).

In the Edit field you can enter data values in HDRs without causing any
interrupts or status changes. This field allows you to change an HDR value
without the HIP status. You can also type CTRL_R (control R) in the Edit
field, but this has no effect unless the HDR is associated with an input
file. (See @45[HIP files].) @43[more]

The middle portion of the HIP Register Window (under STATUS) monitors
the HIP status registers (HSRs). Changes to the Host and 21xx fields in
the top portion of the window are reflected by a change in the HSR values.
The upper two bits of HSR7 are shown separately in the HIP_OVWRITE (bit 7)
field and the HIP_RESET (bit 6) field, as well as in the value of HSR7
itself. These bits control the HIP overwrite mode and software reset.

@43[more]
The bottom portion of the HIP Register Window is a table that shows the
transfer status of each of the HDRs. You cannot directly alter any
values in this table. The columns labeled 0 1 2 3 4 5 refer to the HDRs.
An "e" indicates that the HDR is empty (has been read since the last
update to this HDR). An "f" indicates that the HDR is full (has NOT
been read since the last update to this HDR). The values in the row 
labeled 2111 or msp50 indicate the status of transfers from the ADSP-21xx
and the values in the HOST row indicate the status of transfers from the
host.
@43[first page]
.45
Host Port input/output files
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]
 
You must explicitly assign a file to an HDR and open it before the HDR can read 
or write to it. The input data file contains one hex value per line which will 
be read into the assigned HDR. See also @43[HIP Registers].
.46
CODEC Control Registers       
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This window contains the analog interface registers and fields.
These registers are all memory mapped. These registers may also be 
modified in the @56[data memory window].
The fields in the codec/powerdown window are defined as follows:

Register       Contents

CRECV 		Analog Receive (ADC) Data Register - dm(0x3fed)
CTRAN 		Analog Transmit (DAC) Data Register-  dm(0x3fec)
CRBUF 		Analog Receive Autobuffer Enable - dm(0x3fef) bit 0
CTBUF 		Analog Transmit Autobuffer Enable - dm(0x3fef) bit 1
CRIREG		Analog Receive Autobuffer I Register - dm(0x3fef) bits 4-6
CTIREG		Analog Transmit Autobuffer I Register - dm(0x3fef) bits 9-11
CRMREG		Analog Receive Autobuffer M Register - dm(0x3fef) bits 2-3
CTMREG		Analog Transmit Autobuffer M Register - dm(0x3fef) bits 7-8
@46[more]
PUCR            Power Down Context Reset Enable Bit - dm(0x3fef) bit 12
PDFORCE         Power Down Force Bit - dm(0x3fef) bit 13

The following registers do NOT affect simulation operation in any way.
They are for display purposes only in the simulator.

XTALDELAY       4096 Cycle Delay Enable - dm(0x3f3f) bit 14
XTALDIS         XTAL Pin Drive Enable - dm(0x3fef) bit 15

TPS             Encoder ADC Preamp Select - dm(0x3fee) bit 0  
TMS             Encode ADC Mux Select - dm(0x3fee) bit 1
RG              Decoder (DAC) PGA gain select - dm(0x3fee) bits 2-4
NPWDD           Analog Interface Power Down Digital - dm(0x3fee) bit 5
NPWDA           Analog Interface Power Down Analog - dm(0x3fee) bit 6
ADCBYP          ADC High Pass Filter Bypass  - dm(0x3fee) bit 7
DACBYP          DAC High Pass Filter Bypass  - dm(0x3fee) bit 7

@46[first page]

.48
Boot Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

Boot memory (Boot) is 24 bits wide and spans from address 0 through 0x3fff.
The display contains the memory map access for the location, address, data (in
whatever format), and symbol name, if any. BM can be displayed in hexadecimal,
decimal , and disassembled formats. 

The following @177[special keys] are active for BM windows:
        @60[<Ctrl-D>] Dump Memory
        @61[<Ctrl-M>] Load Memory
        @62[<Ctrl-F>] Fill Memory
        @63[<Ctrl-G>] Go To Memory
        @18[<Ctrl-T>] Toggles Window Display Format
.52
Program Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

Program memory (PM) is 24 bits wide and spans from address 0 through 0x3fff.
The display contains the memory map access for the location, address, data (in
whatever format), and symbol name, if any. PM can be displayed in hexadecimal,
decimal, 1.15 fixed point, and disassembled formats. 

In the disassembled format window, highlighted access fields indicate the
presence of a @169[Multi-Breakpoint]/@168[Breakpoint].  Also note that in the instruction
pointed to by the @31[Program Counter] (PC), the address field is highlighted.  When
an address field is highlighted in the "normal" highlighted color, this 
indicates the instruction will really be executed. 

The following @177[special keys] are active for PM windows:
        @60[<Ctrl-D>] Dump Memory
        @61[<Ctrl-M>] Load Memory
        @62[<Ctrl-F>] Fill Memory
        @63[<Ctrl-G>] Go To Memory
        @18[<Ctrl-T>] Toggles Window Display Format
.56
Data Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

Data memory (DM) is 16 bits wide and spans from address 0 through 0x3fff.
The display contains the memory map access for the location, address, data (in
whatever format), and symbol name, if any.  DM can be displayed in hexadecimal,
and 1.15 fixed point formats.

The following @177[special keys] are active for DM windows:

        @60[<Ctrl-D>] Dump Memory
        @61[<Ctrl-M>] Load Memory
        @62[<Ctrl-F>] Fill Memory
        @63[<Ctrl-G>] Go To Memory
        @18[<Ctrl-T>] Toggles Window Display Format

.60
Dump Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to dump memory data to a file.  You are prompted for an
address range and file name.  The data is dumped in a format consistent with
the format of the window from which it is invoked, ie: hexadecimal, floating
point etc..  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM]
or @48[BM], window.  
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<Ctrl-D>] key.

See also @61[Load Memory].
.61
Load Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to load memory data from a file.  You are prompted for
an address range and file name.  The data is loaded, in a format consistent
with the format of the window from which it is invoked, ie: hexadecimal,
1.15 fixed point ,or decimal, starting at the address supplied.  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM]
or @48[BM], window. 
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<Ctrl-M>] key.

See also @60[Dump Memory].
.62
Fill Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to fill memory with a given pattern.  You are prompted 
for an address range and pattern which will be replicated in memory.  The
pattern is entered in hexadecimal format.  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM]
or @48[BM], window.
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<Ctrl-F>] key.
.63
Go To Address
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to go to a particular memory address.  You are prompted 
for an address.  After entering the address, the window is moved to the given
address.  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM]
or @48[BM], window. 
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<Ctrl-G>] key.
.68
Chip Reset 
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

A full chip reset (with boot) is invoked here. See the @76[Command Window].


.70
Color Display Attributes
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to alter the color attributes of the windowing system of
the simulator.  A pop-up window is displayed with a list of window types and
areas within those windows on the left hand side and the corresponding
color attributes currently in effect.  Upon choosing the item for which a
change in color is desired, another pop-up window is displayed which presents
a pallette of colors.  The current color attributes for the item which was
selected is enclosed by brackets ([]).  Upon choosing a new color selection,
the original pop-up is redisplayed, showing the new color selections. 

Selecting the "Accept" option will redisplay the screen with the new color 
selections.  The "Cancel" option cancels any new color selections made.  The 
"Reset" option resets all colors to the simulator defaults.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

Note that color attributes are saved in window files, either upon @2[exiting] the
simulator or manually saving the @98[window file].  
.71
Floating Point Display Attributes
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to alter the attributes of all floating point displays in
the simulator.  A pop-up window is displayed with a list of attributes and
their current settings.  The current setting can be changed by selecting the
appropriate attribute.  

In order for the new selections to take effect, the "accept changes" option
must be selected after the new attributes have been chosen.

.76
Command Window
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Command Window is used to enter simulator commands. A command history of 20
commands is kept and may be recalled and edited using the arrow keys or the 
mouse. The format for the commands is as follows:

Register Commands

   Function                          Command Syntax

   Set a register to a value         register = expression (ex. ax0=1)

   Examine contents of register      ?register (ex. ?ax0)
   (displayed in command output
   window)

   Undefine register                 UNDEFINE register (ex. UNDEFINE ax0)
                                     U register (ex. U ax0)
@76[more]
Memory Commands

    Set memory to value              FILL addr or range value 
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1) 1234)
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1),pm(10) 1234)
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1)/10 1234)
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1)< 'file')

    Assemble value                   ASSEMBLE addr instruction 
                                     A addr instruction
                                     (ex. assemble pm(0) jump 10)
                                     (ex. A pm(10) ax0=ar)

   Examine contents of               ?addr (ex. ?dm(3) )
   memory

   Dump contents of                  DUMP addr or range [>'file']
   memory                            D addr or range [>'file']
                                     (ex. D dm[0]/0x100 >'d.dat')

   Find value                        FIND range value
                                     F range value
                                     (ex. FIND dm(2),dm(0x100) 0xabcd)

   Load executable file              LOAD 'file'
                                     L 'file'

@76[more]
   Load rom image                    LOADROM 'file'
                                     LR 'file'

   Plot memory                       PLOT range increment
                                     PL range increment
                                     (ex. PL pm[0]/0x100 1)
Break/Watch Commands

    Set a breakpoint                 BREAK addr (ex. BREAK pm(10))
                                     B addr (ex. B pm(10))

    Delete a breakpoint              BD addr or number or all
                                     (ex. BD pm(10)))
                                     (ex. BD 1)
                                     (ex. BD all)

    Set a break expression           BE expression
                                     (ex. BE ax0 == 2) NOTE == not =

    Set a break change               BC expression
                                     (ex. BC ax0)

    Set a break range                BR range
                                     (ex. BR pm[10],pm[20])

    List breakpoints                 B
    to command output window

    Set a watchpoint                 W addr or range
                                     (ex. W pm[10],pm[20])

    Set a watch expression           WE expression
                                     (ex. WE ax0 ==1) NOTE: ==

    Delete watchpoint                WD number
                                     (ex. WD 0)

    List watchpoints                 W
    to command output window

@76[more]
Execution Commands

    Run                              GO
                                     G

    Run to an address                GO addr
                                     G addr
                                     (ex. GO pm[22])

    Step                             STEP
                                     S

    Step 'N' times                   STEP number
                                     S number
                                     (ex. S 12)

    Chip reset (boot if MMAP 0)      CHIPRESET
                                     CR

    Chip reset -- don't boot         RESETNOBOOT
                                     RE

    Execute a single instruction     EXECUTE instruction
                                     EXE instruction
                                     (ex. EXE ar = ay0 + 1)

@76[more]
Symbols and Alias Commands

    Load a symbol file               LS 'file'
                                     (ex. LS 'test')

    Add a user defined symbol        ADDSYMBOL + 'symbol' addr
                                     ASYM + 'symbol' addr
                                     (ex. ADDSYMBOL + 'ggg' pm[3])

    Set an alias                     ALIAS 'new name' 'command'
                                     (ex. ALIAS 'my_reset' 'cr')

    Delete an alias                  DELETE 'new name'
                                     (ex. DELETE 'my_reset')

    List alias to command            ALIAS
    output window

@76[more]
File Related Commands

    Open a port data file            OPENPORT addr < 'filename'
    for reading                      OP addr < 'filename'
                                     (ex. OP pm[12] < 'in.dat')
    
    Open a port data file            OPENPORT addr > 'filename'
    for writing                      OP addr > 'filename'
                                     (ex. OP pm[12] > 'in.dat')

    Open a sport data file           OPENSPORT sport number < 'filename'
    for reading                      OS sport number < 'filename'
                                     (ex. OS 0 < 'in.dat')
    
    Open a sport data file           OPENSPORT sport number > 'filename'
    for writing                      OS sport number > 'filename'
                                     (ex. OS 1 > 'in.dat')

    Open a HIP data file             HIPREAD hdr# > 'filename  (2111+2150)
    for reading                      HIPR hdr# > 'filename  (2111+2150)
                                     (ex. HIPR hdr1 >'myfile') -- the host reads
    
    Open a HIP data file             HIPWRITE hdr# <  'filename  (2111+2150)
    for writing                      HIPW hdr# < 'filename  (2111+2150)
                                     (ex. HIPW hdr1 <'myfile') -- the host writes to 21xx
    
    Open a HIP file for booting      HIPBOOT 'filename' (2111+2150)
                                     HB < 'filename'
@76[more]

    Write a window file              WIN > 'filename'

    Save state to file               STATE > 'filename'

    Restore state from a file        STATE < 'filename'

    Start writing to keystroke       KEYON 'filename'
    file                             KO 'filename'

    Stop writing to keystroke        KEYOFF
    file                             KF

    Read in a keystroke file         < 'filename'

@76[more]
Interrupt Commands

    Specify periodic interrupt       INTERRUPT sig min [max] [OFFSET number]
                                     I sig min [max] [OFFSET number]
                                     (ex. I irq0 100.2 105.4 OFFSET 10)

   Specify periodic flag input       INTERRUPT fi period [ONCE or RESET]
                                     I fi period [ONCE or RESET]
                                     (ex. I fi 10 ONCE)

   Display settings for signal in    INTERRUPT sig
   command output window             I sig
                                     (ex. I irq2)
   
@76[more]
   Display all signal settings       INTERRUPT ALL
                                     I all

   Display all active signal         INTERRUPT ACTIVE
   settings                          I ACTIVE

   Specify periodic hip read         HIPREAD hdr# min [max] [OFFSET number]
   interrupt                         HIPR hdr# min [max] [OFFSET number]
                                     (ex. HIPR hdr4 12.5 13.7 OFFSET 10)

   Specify periodic hip write        HIPWRITE hdr# min [max] [OFFSET number]
   interrupt                         HIPW hdr# min [max] [OFFSET number]
                                     (ex. HIPW hdr4 12.5 13.7 OFFSET 10)


@76[more]
SPORT Commands

    Connect frame syncs              CONNECT rfs# tfs#
				     (ex. CONNECT rfs0 tfs1)

    Disconnect frame syncs           CONNECT rfs# tfs# OPEN
                                     (ex. CONNECT rfs0 tfs1 open)

    Disconnect all frame syncs       CONNECT ALL OPEN

    Display frame sync               CONNECT
    connections in command output
    window
    
    Connect rfs to tfs and dt to     LOOPBACK sport number
    dr in a sport (loop back)        LB sport number
                                     (ex. LB 0)

    Disconnect a loop back           LOOPBACK sport number OPEN
                                     LB sport number OPEN
                                     (ex. LB 0 OPEN)

    Disconnect all loop backs        LOOPBACK ALL OPEN
                                     LB ALL OPEN

@76[more]
Profile Commands

    Add a profile range              PROFILEADD range# addr or range
                                     PA range# addr or range
                                     (ex. PA 0 pm[0],pm[10])

    Delete a profile range           PROFILEDELETE range #
                                     PD range #
                                     (ex. PD 0)

    Clear all profile ranges         PROFILECLEAR
                                     PC

    Reset profile data               PROFILERESET
                                     PR

@76[more]
Miscellaneous Commands            

    Quit the simulator without       QUIT 
    saving a window file             Q (this will prompt: are you sure ?)

    Exit the simulator saving        EXIT
    a window file                    E (this will prompt: are you sure ?)

    Shell out to DOS                 SHELL
                                     SH

    Shell a command to DOS           SHELL 'command'
                                     SH 'command'
                                     (ex. SH 'dir/w')

    Get simulator version            VERSION
				     VS
@76[first page]
.79
Trace Program Execution
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Trace Window captures a history of processor activity during program 
execution. By default the last 10 instructions which were executed are saved.
To change the the size of the trace history, type CTRL_R (control R). A pop up
window will appear asking for the number of frames (instructions) to trace.

There are four fields of information displayed for each instruction shown in the
window. From left to right, these are: program counter value, disassembled code
(instructions) with address labels, data memory read or write performed, and
program memory read or write performed.
.83
2100 Cache Memory
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Cache memory window display the current contents of the on-chip
memory cache. The first field displays the internal cache address. The 
next field displays the 2100 pm memory location which has been
copied to the cache. The last field displays the disassembled memory
at that location

.84
Command Output Window
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Command Output Window displays information requested from the @76[Command Window].
Data is displayed in the same format as selected in the Command Window.
The following data is displayed in the Command Output Window:

   Command                          Output

   ALIAS                            List of current aliases

   BREAK,B                          List of current breakpoints

   CHIPRESET,CR                     Boot Cycles, Boot Page
   BF=1

   Connect                          List of tied frame syncs

   D range                          Contents of memory 

@84[more]
   HIPREAD hdr#                     min,max,offset for interrupt
   HIPR hdr#

   HIPWRITE hdr#                    min,max,offset for interrupt
   HIPW hdr#

   INTERRUPT signal                 min,max,offset for interrupt
   I signal

   I ALL                            min,max,offset for all signals

   I ACTIVE                         min,max,offset for all active
                                    signals

   WATCHPOINT                       list of all active watchpoints
   W

   ? expression                     The value of the expression.

Also, the evaluated current watchpoints are displayed in this
window when running.

@84[first page]
   
   
   
.85
Profile Program Execution
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

An execution profile shows the time spent executing instructions in different
ranges in a program. It allows you to analyze the execution patterns of your 
program. To create a profile, you open the profile window, select parameters, 
enable profiling, then run your program. You can examine results in the profile 
window.

The simulator counts the cycles spent executing instructions in each address
range you identify. The simulator automatically counts cycles for all other
ranges, during IDLE instructions and during multicycle instructions (e.g.,
memory accesses with wait states). These counts are shown in the profile
window next to other, idle , and extra cycle.

Execution time for each range is expressed as a percentage of time period.
The profile covers three periods: short-term, long-term, and cumulative.
The short-term and long-term periods are relative to the current cycle; that
is , a time period of 10 cycles is always the most recent 10 cycles. You choose
@85[more]
the number of instruction (execution) cycles in the short-term and long-term
time periods. The cumulative time period is always the total number of 
execution cycles since you last reset the profile.

The long-term count specifies a number of short-term periods. 

The rightmost column of the profile window, labeled Cum, shows the percent of
the cumulative time spent in each range since you last reset the accumulated
data. The columns labeled STA (short-term average) and LTA (long-term average)
list the percent of the short-term and long-term time periods, respectively,
for each range. The area under the labels 0%%, 25%%, 50%%, 75%%, and 100%%
displays a simple histogram of the information in the Cum, STA, and LTA columns.
@85[first page]

.89
Expression Evaluation
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Expressions Window can display and evaluate expressions while you perform
other debugging actions. The simulator updates an expression's value as it
changes. The ?+ command (See the @76[Command Window])
adds an expression and the ?- command deletes an expression. You can also type
an expression directly into the command window. CTRL_D (control D) will delete
an expression.

Values displayed in the Expressions Window are always displayed as 32 bit sign-
extended numbers.

.91
Break Expression Evaluation
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The simulator can break on the state of an expression. It can break either when
an expression is true or whenever the value of an expression changes. For 
example, you may wish to break execution whenever the expression "AX0 > 3" is 
true. As long as AX0 contains a value less than or equal to three, the 
expression is false. When AX0 becomes loaded with a larger value, however, 
the expression becomes true.

Enter this from the break expression window as "ax0 > 3".
Enter this from the @76[Command Window] as "be ax0 > 3".

NOTE: The "C" language style == is used for comparatives. A single "=" is 
evaluated as an assignment operator by the expression parser and will result 
with an invalid expression.
.93
Defaults Window
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The defaults menu allows you to select beep or echo, and allows you to set the
screen update rate and the keystroke file time-out value. The screen update rate
is the number of cycles which are to be executed before updating the screen
when running. The run timeout is the number of seconds to run (when running
from a keystroke file) before stopping. 

The defaults are saved as part of the window file. See the quit and exit
commands in the @76[Command Window].

.95
Load Executable File
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to load executable files into the simulator.  The 
executable file typically defines @52[Program Memory] (PM) , @48[Boot Memory], or
@56[Data Memory] (DM) and symbolic information.

Note that executable files are loaded independently of any memory map
attributes.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].
.97
Load Window File
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to load window files into the simulator.  The window file
defines video mode, color attributes, floating point attributes, window types,
window sizes and screen positions.  

When a window file is loaded, the current window attributes are replaced with 
those from the file.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @98[Save Window File].
.98
Save Window File
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to create a window file for the simulator.  The window 
file defines video mode, color attributes, floating point attributes, window
types, window sizes and screen positions.  

When a window file is created, the current window attributes are stored in the
filename supplied by the user.  Creating a window file does not affect the
current window attributes in any manner.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @97[load window file].
.99
Start Keystroke Recording
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to create a keystroke file for the simulator.  A
keystroke file is essentially a log of both keystrokes and mouse actions that 
can be created and then "played back".  It is typically used when a set of
actions is, or will be, done repeatedly.  When a keystroke file is created, all
subsequent keystrokes and mouse actions are recorded to the given file, until
the @100[Stop Keystroke Recording] command is issued.  Later, the file can be "played
back" with the @101[Playback Keystroke File] command.

There is something to watch out for when using keystroke files.  Because of the
mechanism used to replay keystroke files, the use of commands that wait for any
keystroke, the run command for example, should be avoided.  What will happen in
this case is that immediately after the run command is issued from the file, 
the next keystroke from the file would be read; the simulator will interpret
this as signal to stop running and would halt the simulation.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @100[Stop Keystroke Recording] and @101[Playback Keystroke File].
.100
Stop Keystroke Recording
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to stop recording keystrokes to a keystroke file.  The 
keystroke file is closed and no further keystrokes or mouse actions are logged
to the file.

There is something to watch out for when using keystroke files.  Because of the
mechanism used to replay keystroke files, the use of commands that wait for any
keystroke, the run command for example, should be avoided.  What will happen in
this case is that immediately after the run command is issued from the file, 
the next keystroke from the file would be read; the simulator will interpret
this as signal to stop running and would halt the simulation.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @99[Start Keystroke Recording] and @101[Playback Keystroke File].
.101
Playback Keystroke File
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to "playback" a keystroke file.  All keystrokes and mouse
actions are repeated in exactly the same order in which the keystroke file was
created.  

There is something to watch out for when using keystroke files.  Because of the
mechanism used to replay keystroke files, the use of commands that wait for any
keystroke, the run command for example, should be avoided.  What will happen in
this case is that immediately after the run command is issued from the file, 
the next keystroke from the file would be read; the simulator will interpret
this as signal to stop running and would halt the simulation.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @99[Start Keystroke Recording] and @100[Stop Keystroke Recording].
.102
Display File
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to display a text file.  When invoked, the user is 
prompted for a filename.  The file is then loaded into the simulator and
displayed.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  
.103
Operating System Shell
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to create an operating system shell.  

For DOS platforms, the simulator is suspended and the user is transferred to
an operating system shell.  Typing 'exit' from the shell will return the
user to the simulator.

For Unix platforms, an 'xterm' is spawned.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  
.104
Operating System Shell Command
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to create an operating system shell and execute an
OS command.  

For DOS platforms, the simulator is suspended and the command is executed 
in  the operating system shell.  When the command is complete, control
transfers back to the simulator.

For Unix platforms, an 'xterm' is spawned.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar] or
by using the SH 'command' command from the @76[Command Window].
.105
PC Stack
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of the Program Counter (PC) stack.  The PC stack is 14 bits
wide and 16 locations deep. 
The stack generates an overflow signal (PC_O) when the stack is
overflowed and an empty signal (PC_E) when no locations in the stack are
occupied.  PC_O and PC_E are stored in the @33[SSTAT] register.  
.106
Status Stack
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of the Status Stack.  The Status Stack is 7 locations deep
for the 2100 and 2101. It is 9 locations deep for the 2111 and 12 locations 
deep for the 2150. It contains the pushed contents of the imask, mstat, and
sstat registers. See @33[Status registers] and @35[Interrupt Registers]
.107
Loop Counter Stack
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of the Loop Counter Stack.  The Loop Counter Stack is 4 
locations deep and 14 bits wide.  The Loop Counter Stack is synchronized with
the @108[Loop Address Stack].  They will always contain the same number of active
locations.  The stack generates an overflow signal (LS_O) when the stack is
overflowed and an empty signal (LS_E) when no locations in the stack are
occupied.  LS_O and LS_E are stored in the @33[SSTAT] register.  

See also the @108[Loop Address Stack] display.
.108
Loop Address Stack
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of the Loop Address Stack.  The Loop Address Stack is 4 
locations deep and 18 bits wide.  Each entry in the stack contains a 14 bit
loop termination address and a 4 bit loop termination code.

           Bit        Description
           0-13       Loop termination address
           14-17      Loop termination code (See User's Manual)

The Loop Address Stack is synchronized with the @107[Loop Counter Stack].  They will
always contain the same number of active locations.  The stack generates an 
overflow signal (LS_O) when the stack is overflowed and an empty signal (LS_E)
when no locations in the stack are occupied.  LS_E and LS_O are stored in the
@33[SSTAT] register.  

See also the @107[Loop Counter Stack] display. 
.109
PM Symbol Display
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of all @52[Program Memory] (PM) symbols.  The symbols can be 
displayed in either alphabetical or numeric order, according to the selection 
from the @1[menu bar].  The display shows the symbolic name and the address of the
symbol.  Symbols are loaded into the simulator via the 
@95[Load Executable File]  and load symbols function. See the @76[Command Window].

At present, only global and static symbols are recognized.

See also @113[Delete Symbols] , @114[Data Memory (DM) Symbol Display] , 
and @111[Boot Memory (BM) Symbol Display].   
.111
BM Symbol Display
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of all @48[Program Memory] (BM) symbols.  The symbols can be 
displayed in either alphabetical or numeric order, according to the selection 
from the @1[menu bar].  The display shows the symbolic name and the address of the
symbol.  Symbols are loaded into the simulator via the 
@95[Load Executable File]  and load symbols function. See the @76[Command Window].

At present, only global and static symbols are recognized.

See also @113[Delete Symbols] , @114[Data Memory (DM) Symbol Display] , 
and @111[Boot Memory (BM) Symbol Display].   
.113
Delete Symbols
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function will delete all symbols from the simulator.

See also @114[Data Memory (DM) Symbol Display] , @109[Program Memory Symbol Display]
and @111[Boot Memory (BM) Symbol Display].   
.114
DM Symbol Display
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This is the display of all @56[Data Memory] (DM) symbols.  The symbols can be 
displayed in either alphabetical or numeric order, according to the selection 
from the @1[menu bar].  The display shows the symbolic name and the address of the
symbol.  Symbols are loaded into the simulator via the 
@95[Load Executable File]  and load symbols function.

At present, only global and static symbols are recognized.

See also @113[Delete Symbols] , @114[Data Memory (DM) Symbol Display] , 
and @111[Boot Memory (BM) Symbol Display].   
.116
Step
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to single step the simulator.  A single step will take
from 1 to 16 clock cycles, due to memory wait states etc..  The display is 
automatically updated after each step.

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<F10>] key.

See also the @117[Step 'n' Times] and @118[Run] commands.
.117
Step 'n' Times
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to execute 'n' single steps of the simulator.  A single
step will take from 1 to 16 clock cycles, due to memory wait states etc..
This function will halt upon reaching a breakpoint, or pressing any key.

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<Shift-F10>] key.
When this function is activated a pop-up will appear requesting the number of
steps to execute.

See also the @116[Step] and @118[Run] commands.
.118
Run
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to begin continuous execution of the simulator.  The 
simulator will continue executing until one of the following occur:

             - An instruction at which a breakpoint is set was about to be
                 executed (ie: PC = <breakpoint address>).

             - A keystroke has been detected.  

             - An error has been detected.

The simulator will automatically update the display after approximately every
1000 instruction cycles. (See the @93[defaults window])

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<F4>] key.

See also the @117[Step 'n' Times] and @116[Step] commands.
.120
CBUG Index
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

                    @142[Assign]             @148[Locals]
                    @150[Backtrace]          @127[Next]
                    @132[Break]              @129[Next 'n' Times]
                    @124[Continue]           @149[Params]
                    @133[Delete]             @139[Print]
                    @121[Display]            @123[Restart]
