.121
CBUG Display
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Display command creates a CBUG Display window which displays an
expression and its value in  4 data formats. CBUG Display
windows remain on-screen until deleted manually using the <Esc> key,
and can be moved and re-sized like other emulator/simulator window.

The expression is evaluated in the current scoping context, and if any
expression components go out of context (by use of the @151[Up] and @152[Down]
commands, or by program execution), an "out of context" message is
displayed.

Related CBUG "Data" menu choices are:
        @139[Print]    - Evaluate and print an expression
        @142[Assign]   - Alter the value of a variable
        @121[Display]  - Monitor expression values
        @143[Where Is] - Describe where a symbol is stored
.123
CBUG Restart
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Restart command resets the emulator/simulator and starts executing code.
This is analogous to issuing the emulator/simulator Chip Reset and @118[Run] commandsAfter the Restart command, CBUG runs until it is stopped. Use the Restart command when
you want to simulate a reboot of your system.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.124
CBUG Continue
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Continue command causes CBUG to resume execution from the current
program counter. After the Continue command, CBUG runs until it is
stopped.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.125
CBUG Go Until
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Go-Until command sets a self-deleting CBUG @132[breakpoint] at a
specified location and resumes execution as in the Continue command.
The break location is set in a pop-up window. The Go-Until command
will run until it hits the breakpoint or is otherwise stopped.

The Go-Until command can be used for shortcut breakpoint operations
such as running to a function (specify a function name), finishing a
loop (specify a line number outside a loop), executing one iteration
of a loop (specify a line number inside a loop), etc.
 
Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.126
CBUG Step
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Step command executes one line of C source code. Function calls
are followed; if a function call is executed, the called function is
entered.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.127
CBUG Next
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Next command is similar to Step, but the entire expression
(including all function calls and assignments) is executed without
stopping. Execution stops when control reaches the next line of code
in the same function which was executing when the Next command was
given; function calls are not followed.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.128
CBUG Step 'n' Times
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Step 'n' Times command steps the execution of your program as in
the Step command, but does so n times. A pop-up window is presented
for entering the count. If a breakpoint is encountered (or execution
is otherwise stopped), Step 'n' Times stops before taking all n steps.
Function calls are followed.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.129
CBUG Next 'n' Times
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Next 'n' Times executes code as in Next, but it does so n times. A
pop-up window is presented for entering the repeat count. If a
@132[breakpoint] is encountered (or execution is otherwise stopped), Next
'n' Times stops before taking all n steps. Function calls are not
followed.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.130
CBUG Finish
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Finish command resumes execution and runs until the
currently-scoped function returns or execution is stopped. If the @151[Up]
and @152[Down] commands have changed the scoping context, the Finish command
finishes from the changed context. The Finish command is useful when
you want to complete the execution of a function and return to its
parent.

Related CBUG "Execution" menu choices are:
        @126[Step]           - Execute one C line; follow calls
        @127[Next]           - Execute one C line; do not follow calls
        @128[Step 'n' Times] - Step that many times
        @129[Next 'n' Times] - Next that many times
        @124[Continue]       - Resume execution (await stop or halt)
        @125[Go Until]       - Continue and stop at a specified line number
        @130[Finish]         - Continue until back in calling function
        @123[Restart]        - Execute a @68[chip reset] and Continue
.132
CBUG Break
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Break command sets a breakpoint. A line number is specified in a
pop-up window. 

Breakpoints are preserved whenever the program is @123[restarted].

You can set a breakpoint on a single line by double-clicking on that
line.

Related CBUG "Breaks" menu choices are:
        @132[Break]     - Add a CBUG breakpoint
        @133[Delete]    - Remove a CBUG breakpoint
        @134[List]      - Describe CBUG breakpoints and their status
.133
CBUG Delete
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Delete command deletes a breakpoint. A pop-up window requests the
breakpoint number(s) or "all" to delete from the breakpoint list.

You can delete a breakpoint on a single line by double-clicking on
that line.

Related CBUG "Breaks" menu choices are:
        @132[Break]     - Add a CBUG breakpoint
        @133[Delete]    - Remove a CBUG breakpoint
        @134[List]      - Describe CBUG breakpoints and their status
.134
CBUG List
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The List command lists all CBUG breakpoints, showing their number,
and where in the program they are. 

Related CBUG "Breaks" menu choices are:
        @132[Break]     - Add a CBUG breakpoint
        @133[Delete]    - Remove a CBUG breakpoint
        @134[List]      - Describe CBUG breakpoints and their status
.139
CBUG Print
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

Data values (and assignments) may be printed to the Status
display using the Print command. 

Related CBUG "Data" menu choices are:
        @139[Print]    - Evaluate and print a data value
        @142[Assign]   - Alter the value of a variable
        @121[Display]  - Monitor data values
        @143[Where Is] - Describe where a symbol is stored
.142
CBUG Assign
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Assign command sets the value of variables. The assignment
statement is entered in a pop-up window. 

The variables are evaluated with respect to the current scoping
context. You may not use Assign to evaluate a function call.

Type compatibility and bounds checking are not performed by CBUG
variable assignments. Display expressions are updated after each
command assigned.

Related CBUG "Data" menu choices are:
        @139[Print]    - Evaluate and print an expression
        @142[Assign]   - Alter the value of a variable
        @121[Display]  - Monitor expression values
        @143[Where Is] - Describe where a symbol is stored
.143
CBUG Where Is
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Whereis command provides information about the storage allocation
for a symbol. The symbol is entered into a pop-up window. The Whereis
command displays the register number, frame pointer offset, or
absolute address and memory space (PM vs. DM) of the symbol's data,
as appropriate, in the Status display.

Related CBUG "Data" menu choices are:
        @139[Print]    - Evaluate and print an data value
        @142[Assign]   - Alter the value of a variable
        @121[Display]  - Monitor data values 
        @143[Where Is] - Describe where a symbol is stored
.144
CBUG What Is
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Whatis command displays the data type of an expression or
variable. The expression or variable is entered into a pop-up window.
Results are displayed in the Status display.

Related CBUG "Data" menu choices are:
        @139[Print]    - Evaluate and print an expression
        @142[Assign]   - Alter the value of a variable
        @121[Display]  - Monitor expression values
        @143[Where Is] - Describe where a symbol is stored
.146
CBUG Stack
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Stack command presents C run-time stack information relative to
the current scoping context in the Status display, namely:

        - nesting level, function name, source file and line number
        - current, parent, and child frame pointers (as appropriate)
        - parameter names and values

Refer to the ADSP-2100 Family C Programmer's Manual for detailed
information about the C run-time stack model.

Related CBUG "Context" menu choices are:
        @146[Stack]     - Report info on the current stack frame
        @150[Backtrace] - Report the function call tree
        @151[Up]        - Change the scoping context up one level
        @152[Down]      - Change the scoping context down one level
.148
CBUG Locals
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Locals command lists all local variables of the currently scoped
function.

Related CBUG "Symbols" menu choices are:
        @154[Functions] - List functions
        @155[Globals]   - List global variables
        @148[Locals]    - List local variables
        @149[Params]    - List function parameters
.149
CBUG Params
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Params command lists all parameter variables of the currently
scoped function.

Related CBUG "Symbols" menu choices are:
        @154[Functions] - List functions
        @155[Globals]   - List global variables
        @148[Locals]    - List local variables
        @149[Params]    - List function parameters
.150
CBUG Backtrace
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Backtrace command displays the calling sequence in the Status
display, showing how the program reached the currently-executing
function. The sequence starts with main() and ends with the function
that is in the current execution context. Each entry in the report
shows the function's

        - frame level
        - program counter value (return address)
        - function name 
        - parameter names and values
        - source file name 
        - line number	

Related CBUG "Context" menu choices are:
        @146[Stack]     - Report info on the current stack frame
        @150[Backtrace] - Report the function call tree
        @151[Up]        - Change the scoping context up one level
        @152[Down]      - Change the scoping context down one level
.151
CBUG Up
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Up command temporarily changes the current scoping context up one
level (to the function's parent) without changing the execution
context. The Source and CBUG Display windows are updated to reflect
the new scoping context.

The Up command reports to the Status display the new scoping context,
and its associated program counter value (if not at the start of the
function), function name and its parameter list and values, and its
source file name and line number.

Related CBUG "Context" menu choices are:
        @146[Stack]     - Report info on the current stack frame
        @150[Backtrace] - Report the function call tree
        @151[Up]        - Change the scoping context up one level
        @152[Down]      - Change the scoping context down one level
.152
CBUG Down
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Down command temporarily changes the current scoping context down
one level without changing the execution context. The Source and CBUG
Display windows are updated to reflect the new scoping context.

The Down command reports to the Status display the new scoping
context, and its associated program counter value (if not at the start
of the function), function name and its parameter list and values, and
its source file name and line number.

Related CBUG "Context" menu choices are:
        @146[Stack]     - Report info on the current stack frame
        @150[Backtrace] - Report the function call tree
        @151[Up]        - Change the scoping context up one level
        @152[Down]      - Change the scoping context down one level
.154
CBUG Functions
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Functions command lists the names and data types of all debuggable
functions.

Related CBUG "Symbols" menu choices are:
        @154[Functions] - List functions
        @155[Globals]   - List global variables
        @148[Locals]    - List local variables
        @149[Params]    - List function parameters
.155
CBUG Globals
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The Globals command lists the names and data types of all non-local
variables.

Related CBUG "Symbols" menu choices are:
        @154[Functions] - List functions
        @155[Globals]   - List global variables
        @148[Locals]    - List local variables
        @149[Params]    - List function parameters
.163
Port Close
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to close a memory port.  A pop-up window is displayed
showing the status of all opened ports.  A port can then be selected with the
mouse or arrow keys.  The selected port is then closed.  When a port is closed,
it's file buffers are flushed and the file(s) itself is closed.  Attempted
accesses to closed port results in an error.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also the @164[Port Open] and @165[Port Display] commands.
.164
Port Open
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to open a memory port.  A memory port simulates input
and/or output to a device by reading and/or writing data to/from data files.  
When a port is opened, information is supplied via a pop-up window that will
characterize the functionality of the port.  

The memory type and address of the port are required.  Note that the memory map
must declare the particular memory location to be a port.  The file format is
specified.  This describes how the simulator will read/write data to/from the
file.  For ports that read from the file, the Autowrap attribute specifies
whether or not the file pointer will be wrapped back to the top of the file
when an End-Of-File (EOF) condition is detected.  This attribute is typically
enabled for periodic data.  The next 2 attributes Input and Output Filename
identify the file(s) associated with the port.  If given an input file, any
reads from the memory location will be read from the file.  If given an output
file, any writes to the memory location will be written to the file.  If both
an input file and output file are given, the location can be read from and
written to.  
@164[more]

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also the @163[Port Close] and @165[Port Display] commands.
.165
Port Display
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to display a list of all opened ports and attributes
associated with each port.  No changes may be made to any port via this 
function.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also the @164[Port Open] and @163[Port Close] commands.
.168
Set/Clear Breakpoint
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to set or clear a breakpoint.  A breakpoint is an address
at which the simulator will stop executing immediately prior to the execution 
of the specified address.  

This function is only available when employed from the context of a Program
Memory (PM) window in the disassembled format.  Breakpoints are displayed in
the disassembled PM window.  They are indicated by the highlighting of the 
access field at the breakpoint address.  Use of this function will toggle the
state of a breakpoint at the address at which the cursor is present; if a
breakpoint is not set at that address, one will be established; if a breakpoint
is set at that address, it will be cleared.

Note that once a breakpoint is "hit", it remains active. 

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<F9>] key.

See also @169[Set/Clear Multi-Breakpoint], @170[Display Bkpt] and @171[Clear All Breakpoints].  
.169
Set/Clear Multi-Breakpoint
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to set or clear a multi-breakpoint.  A multi-breakpoint
is an address at which the simulator will stop executing immediately prior to
the 'nth' execution of the specified address.  When invoked, the user is
prompted for the value of 'n'.  

This function is only available when employed from the context of a Program
Memory (PM) window in the disassembled format.  Breakpoints are displayed in
the disassembled PM window.  They are indicated by the highlighting of the 
access field at the breakpoint address.  Use of this function will toggle the
state of a breakpoint at the address at which the cursor is present; if a
breakpoint is not set at that address, one will be established; if a breakpoint
is set at that address, it will be cleared.

Note that once a multi-breakpoint is "hit", it remains active and it's count
is reset to 'n'.

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @177[<F9>] key.

See also @168[Set/Clear Breakpoint], @170[Display Bkpt] and @171[Clear All Breakpoints].  
.170
Display Breakpoints
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to display breakpoints.  When invoked, this function 
produces a pop-up window on the display.  The display contains the occurrence
and the address (or symbol name if one exists at the address).  The occurrence
specifies when a simulation would be halted relative to the execution of this
instruction.  A value of "1" indicates execution would be halted immediately
prior to the first time this instruction would be executed.  A value of "2" 
indicated execution would be halted immediately prior to the second time this
instruction would be executed.  Multi-Breakpoints typically have numbers other
than "1" in this field, whereas a "regular" breakpoint always has a "1" in this
field.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].

See also @169[Set/Clear Multi-Breakpoint], @168[Set/Clear Breakpoint] and
@171[Clear All Breakpoints].  
.171
Clear All Breakpoints
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

This function is used to clear all breakpoints in the simulator.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].

See also @169[Set/Clear Multi-Breakpoint], @168[Set/Clear Breakpoint] and
@170[Display Breakpoints].  
.174
Interrupt Timing
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The interrupt timing window allows you to set the interrupt rate for the
IRQ pins. The min and max fields allow you to enter a range. Basically,
if min does not equal max, the interrupt will occur at random intervals
between min and max. The offset field allows you to start the interrupts
some number of cycles in the future.


.175
Help on Help
     @0[Return to Simulator]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @178[General Info]

The on-line help system of the ADSP-2100 Family Simulators is designed to be 
easy to understand and simple to use.  The help system is also context 
sensitive.  This means that the help system is sensitive to what you are doing,
or trying to do. 

You can get help at any time by pressing the <F1> key.  The screen will then
display a help message pertinent to whatever window you were working in.  Once
the help system is entered, you can choose to remain in the help system or
return to the simulator.  

At the top of the screen, and in the ASCII of many help screens, are words that 
are, typically, in a different color.  These are called keywords and provide an
easy and efficient means of moving throughout the help system.  Selecting these
keywords, either by clicking on them with the mouse or using the arrow keys to 
choose one and pressing <ENTER>, will switch from the current help screen to 
a help screen containing help on your chosen keyword.  Pressing the <ESC> key 
anywhere in the help system will always return you to the simulator.  

Note: The arrow keys will scroll the help screens where appropriate.
.176
